Version 2.2.0 brings a couple of new features and some minor bugfixes
and improvements.

Features:
- New 'PolyD' key assignment mode, which is a Poly mode where two
  voices are allocated at each note on.
  - The new mode integrates seamlessly with the existing modes,
    meaning that as before changing the key assignment parameter
    won't result in any difference to the allocation of voices
    until new voices are allocated.
  - Two new parameters:
    - Unison spread: Sets the spread of the two-voice unison
      in the stereo image, from mono (0) to full L-R stereo (10).
      The Pan Spread parameter still comes into play adding some
      per-voice randomness to the pan position.
    - Unison detune: Sets the pitch spread of the two-voice unison.
      The left voice will be detuned down and the right voice detuned
      up such that the difference between the voices in semitones
      corresponds to the parameter setting (range: 0..1).
    - In conjunction with this, the key assignment parameters have
      moved around, so that the Dual Spread and Dual Detune parameters
      are page #1, and the often little used envelope and retrigger
      parameters have been relegated to the new page #3. Page #2 is
      unchanged.
  - Note that in PolyD mode, each new note uses two voices, which
    effectively halves the voice count in terms of polyphony.
    - A special case is when the Voice Count parameter has an odd value.
      In this case, the key assignment algorithm tries to play as many
      doubled-up voices as possible, while still retaining maximum
      polyphony. This means that there will be notes played which only
      have one voice playing. While slightly quirky, this allows for
      maximum polyphony on resource starved systems (such as the Raspberry
      Pi 3, which allows a maximum of 5 voices in practice (with oversampling
      enabled). It can be alleviated simply by setting the Voice Count to
      an even value.
- Additional LFO: Modulator #3. Identical to modulators 1 and 2, except
  that the Wave parameter has been replaced with a Shape control,
  allowing fine-grained control over the waveform:
  - Parameter values 0..5 offer continuous sweeping from sawtooth (0)
    to triangle (2.5) to reverse sawtooth (5).
  - Parameter values 5..10 offer continuous sweeping from narrow pulse
    (just over 5) to square (7.5) to inverted narrow pulse (10). At
    the extremes (5 and 10), the output flatlines.
  - There is no S/H waveform.
- New Controller menu, allowing direct control for the same modulation
  destinations used for the LFOs from Mod Wheel and After Touch.
  - New parameters: Amount and Destination, for both sources.
    - The Amount parameter is bipolar so that either an increase or
      decrease of the destination can be set.

Bugfixes:
- The limiting function shape control for the sawtooth didn't work
  properly, not capping the width of the resulting sawtooth when
  it got narrow enough at high shape settings. This would lead
  to the oscillator output decreasing more than intended, and for
  oscillator 2, severe digital artifacts in the form of aliasing
  and clicking could occur at high shape settings.
- Under specific conditions, when switching between Poly and Mono modes
  with notes held, notes could hang. This has been fixed.

Improvements:
- Clean up code in some places, removing unused code sections
  and variables.
- Minor CPU improvements in the TPT filter implementation
  (doubles were used where there were no advantages compared to floats).
  This results in a minor decrease in CPU load in this version compared to
  earlier versions, despite the addition of another LFO.

Presets:
- Several new presets have been added, some being general additions,
  and some showcasing the new features introduced in this version.
- A couple of presets have been improved.
- Inadvertent underlines have been removed from the preset names of a
  couple of presets.
- The Voice Count for all factory parameters has been increased from 6
  to 12, in the interest of supporting the new PolyD (Dual) mode without
  the polyphony dropping to 3, while at the same time keeping the required DSP
  resources within the empirical limit of 19 voices (with oversampling) on a
  Raspberry Pi4 Zynthian. (For older Raspberry Pi3 based Zynthians, the limit
  is around 5 voices (with oversampling), so we were already over that limit
  with the old default of 6 voices.)

As usual, a companion version 2.2.0z has been tagged on the Zynthian branch,
which supports the graphic envelope and filter display features in the Zynthian
UI.
